# rlhanne.msg - Hanne Voss, landlady of the boarding house and madam of the Cesspit (Vault City)
# Plain ASCII only: the English Fallout 2 fonts have no umlauts or typographic quotes.

# As madam: report and payout
{100}{}{The sheets are clean, the stairs are dark and nobody upstairs has seen anything. Here's the week.}
{121}{}{. Citizen money. It smells of disinfectant.}
{122}{}{Nothing. The silence ate it. It always does in this city.}

# Prices and share
{130}{}{They come down from Downtown with passes that say they were never here. They pay for that. The more we charge, the more they believe it.}
{140}{}{Our people keep }
{141}{}{ percent of what they bring in. Outsiders don't get paid at all in Vault City. They get tolerated.}
{145}{}{A quarter. (Hanne folds a sheet with sharp corners.) The city takes everything from outsiders. Now we do too.}
{146}{}{A third. More than any outsider earns up there. Less than the silence is worth.}
{147}{}{Almost half? (She looks at you as if you've spilled something.) They'll think you're a fool. Then they'll stay.}

# Morale
{150}{}{They walk upstairs like people who belong in this city. Nobody here has ever seen that before.}
{151}{}{They do what's asked and forget it by morning. That's the job, in Vault City.}
{152}{}{They flinch when a guard walks past the window. Everybody here knows about the Servant Allocation Center.}
{153}{}{One of them is gone. She took the night caravan. Anything is better than being a servant, she said.}

# Crisis: the drug
{160}{}{One of ours is on Jet. The citizens bring it down with them. They take their own medicine and leave us the leftovers.}
{165}{}{(Hanne watches from the door.) Vault City has the best doctors in the wasteland. None of them would have come down those stairs.}
{166}{}{There's an outsider doctor in the Courtyard who asks no questions. It costs, but he's good.}
{167}{}{(Hanne's mouth becomes a thin line.) Jet instead of wages. That's what the servant center would do. I'll write it down. I write everything down.}
{168}{}{Out, then. The Garde will find her by the gate, and then it's the Servant Allocation Center.}

# Look
{200}{}{A severe woman in a spotless apron, a ring of keys at her belt.}
{201}{}{Hanne Voss. A widow from outside who runs a respectable boarding house in the Courtyard. The cellar is less respectable.}

# Construction and staff (her voice)
{460}{}{What do we build? Nothing anyone can see from the street. The upstairs stays a boarding house. Everything else goes under it.}
{463}{}{Something you only get in Vault City.}
{483}{}{Nobody's looking for new faces.}
{484}{}{Our recruiter waits at the Courtyard gate. Outsiders who were refused a day pass. There are always more.}
{485}{}{Our recruiter buys up outsiders' debts at the Amenities Office. They come down the stairs because the alternative is the servant center.}
{490}{}{I know every outsider in the Courtyard. The ones who want a roof and a lock will come.}
{491}{}{(Hanne writes something down.) Debts from the Amenities Office. So we're the servant center now, just cheaper. I'll keep the list.}

# Before the takeover: a cellar in the Courtyard
{700}{}{(A severe woman in a spotless apron blocks the stairs.) Hanne Voss. This is a respectable boarding house. Rooms by the week, no drinking, no outsiders after dark. What happens in my cellar is none of your business.}
{701}{}{(Hanne dries her hands on her apron.) We have an agreement. Now you need papers. Nobody in Vault City keeps a cellar without a permit, least of all this one.}
{702}{}{I know who comes down to your cellar at night.}
{703}{}{[Speech] Let me run your cellar properly. Ten percent is yours, and you never have to see who comes.}
{704}{}{I'll buy the house. $1500.}
{705}{}{I'm a citizen of Vault City. That's all the permit I need.}
{706}{}{[Science] I'll draw up the permit myself. Nobody reads past the first line.}
{707}{}{Where would I get papers?}
{708}{}{Good day, Mrs. Voss.}
{709}{}{Cellar?}
{710}{}{(She looks at you for a long time.) Citizens. With day passes in the wrong direction. Doctors, clerks, a councilor's nephew. The first one was Sorensen from the Amenities Office. I don't ask. I rent rooms, and I wash the sheets.}
{711}{}{Let's talk about the house.}
{720}{}{(She considers it, lips pressed together.) Ten percent. And my name is never in any of it. If the Garde comes, I'm a widow who rents rooms and never goes to the cellar.}
{721}{}{(She counts the money twice, then puts it in her apron.) The house is yours. I stay, as the landlady. Nobody would believe the house without me.}
{730}{}{(She studies your citizen's papers and hands them back.) A citizen. They'll lower their voices when they talk about us. That's worth fifty a week in silence.}
{731}{}{(Two evenings later there's a permit on her kitchen table: "boarding house, with storage cellar", stamped by an office that may or may not exist.) Good work. I've seen worse on real ones.}
{732}{}{Sorensen stamps permits for a living. He also comes down my stairs every Thursday. He's downstairs now. Ask him how much a stamp costs when a man has something to lose.}
{733}{}{Sorensen used to stamp them. Sorensen is dead. Now it's a citizen's word or a forger's hand.}
{790}{}{(Hanne unhooks a key from her belt and hands it to you.) The cellar is yours. I keep the house. We never talk about it upstairs.}
{791}{}{Show me how the cellar is doing.}

# As madam: the raid, the arrested, the gate, Lynette
{850}{}{Close the cellar this week. Nobody comes down.}
{851}{}{The cellar is locked and the girls are at the laundry. When the Garde comes, they'll find storage and dust.}
{860}{}{Our people in the Corrections Center.}
{863}{}{They took }
{864}{}{ of ours to the Corrections Center. In a week they'll be at the Servant Allocation Center, and then they belong to the city. Sorensen could buy them out. Or someone could break them out.}
{865}{}{[Sneak, Lockpick] I'll break them out myself.}
{866}{}{There's nothing we can do. Let them go.}
{867}{}{(They come back through the maintenance tunnels before dawn, in stolen jumpsuits. The Garde will be looking for whoever did it for a long time.)}
{868}{}{(Hanne writes their names in her book and closes it.) Word will get around. In every one of your houses, somebody will ask whether you'd let them go too.}
{870}{}{We need an eye at the gate.}
{871}{}{There's a guard at the Courtyard gate with a sister in the Courtyard and a debt in Downtown. For fifty a week he'd tell us when the Garde is coming.}
{872}{}{[Charisma] Let me talk to him.}
{873}{}{(The guard looks at the floor while you talk, then at the money.) Fifty a week. When the Garde plans a raid in the Courtyard, we'll know a week ahead.}
{880}{}{Use the File. One of Lynette's friends on the council comes down here.}
{881}{}{(A copy of three pages from the File goes to the First Citizen's office in an unmarked envelope. The raids stop. The Garde still stares at the boarding house, but the orders never come. Lynette will not forget the name on those pages. Or yours.)}

